title: 'C-c r c : skg-stage-and-commit'
pid: 9f0e5984-5591-46f9-b5cb-ee68cc042355
body: |2-
  C-c r c commits every node whose changes are all processed, except one
  that needs a node whose changes are not. A node's changes are its own
  (its text, aliases, extra IDs, flags, home skgrepo, its existence) and
  those of its lists, so a child's ~ is its container's change. A node
  goes in whole, all its '.skg' files or none, in each owned skgrepo
  (one git-commit per skgrepo); foreign skgrepos are never committed. A
  processed node waits, uncommitted and still marked, while it needs a
  node that does not go in: it names a new node that does not, it is
  deleted and a node that does not go in still names it, or it is part
  of a merge whose other parts do not all go in.

  A node one of whose files, at HEAD or now, is not what skg writes for
  it (another field order, say, which only a hand edit makes) is not
  processed, so C-c r c leaves it out, however many of its marks are
  set: its file may have changed in a way the diff view does not show,
  and no mark covers. Such a node goes in only with C-c r C, or with
  Git.

  It opens the buffer "*skg commit message*", in which you write the
  commit message, as in magit. There C-c C-c commits with that message,
  and C-c C-k cancels and kills the buffer; either one restores its
  window. The header line says which commit C-c C-c makes: the buffer
  remembers whether C-c r c or C-c r C opened it last. The message is
  sent as written, less its trailing whitespace; a blank one is
  refused. Nothing is asked. The line shown afterward says what went in
  and what is left, and names each processed node that waits and what
  it waits for: "Committed 2 nodes, in public (9c1e40d), private
  (41aa07f). 2 changed nodes are left; 1 of them is processed but waits:
  'Plans for spring' names 'Seed order', which is new and does not go
  in." It names each node left because it changed in a way no mark
  covers, and how: "...; 1 of them changed in a way no mark covers,
  which C-c r C or Git can commit: 'Spring' (its file changed in a way
  the diff view does not show (formatting, say))." When nothing is processed it says
  "Nothing is processed, so nothing was committed; C-c r C commits
  everything."

  After C-c C-c the buffer keeps the message until it is moot: once
  something is committed, or nothing had changed and nothing failed,
  the buffer is killed. When nothing is committed otherwise (nothing
  processed, everything processed waiting, a "yes" declined, a
  refusal, an error), the message stays there, and C-c r c or C-c r C
  reopens it. What was staged in git by hand never goes in, and stays
  staged: a commit is HEAD's tree plus the committed nodes' files. Every
  open view must be saved or reverted first. Afterward the open buffers
  of the diff view are rerendered: each keeps its roots, its folding and
  what it made editable, and what was committed no longer shows as a
  change. The commit lapses the marks of everything it committed; every
  other mark stays.
